Finding: secp256r1 precompile uses a deprecated function
Severity: Informational
Difficulty: Undetermined
Type: Cryptography

Description:
The public key validation depends on the IsOnCurve primitive, which was recently
deprecated in golang 1.21.

The current implementation of the secp256r1 precompile verifies that the values provided
are part of the relevant elliptic curve:

 func newPublicKey(x, y *big.Int) *ecdsa.PublicKey {
 // Check if the given coordinates are valid
 if x == nil || y == nil || !elliptic.P256().IsOnCurve(x, y) {
 return nil
 }
 …
 }

 Figure 2.1: Header of the newPublicKey function

However, the latest version of the IsOnCurve function contains a deprecation note:

 // IsOnCurve reports whether the given (x,y) lies on the curve.
 //
 // Deprecated: this is a low-level unsafe API. For ECDH, use the crypto/ecdh
 // package. The NewPublicKey methods of NIST curves in crypto/ecdh accept
 // the same encoding as the Unmarshal function, and perform on-curve checks.
 IsOnCurve(x, y *big.Int) bool

 Figure 2.2: Deprecated comment on the IsOnCurve method

Recommendations:
Short term, document usage of a deprecated method in the precompile code and
documentation.

Long term, review the usage of deprecated API across the codebase.

