Groth and Shoup proved the security of additive key derivation using hashing in the
random oracle model. Although SHA-3 behaves like a random oracle in the sense of
indifferentiability, a dedicated KDF, like HKDF, has more practical advantages over using a
hash function. More precisely, not all hash functions behave like random oracles in general
or for the purpose of key derivation in particular. As an example, swapping the hash
function for another function like SHA-256 would be problematic.

