multiple connections to amplify resource exhaustion.

The server reads incoming messages, which are then parsed and dispatched to the
appropriate processing, assuming the message comes from a data-carrying packet. The
server reads the first four bytes and then creates a byte vector of the prescribed length, as
figure 8.1 shows.

To exhaust a server’s memory, a malicious client can set the length to an arbitrarily large
value. However, successful resource exhaustion will require the adversary to send enough
data to fill memory. The attack is also constrained to a short time window that depends on
the current network task. However, depending on the platform, Rust will not commit to this
memory unless it is written to. Furthermore, each network operation is part of a network
“task,” which should time out after the preconfigured amount of time.

Nevertheless, the attack is rather straightforward for an attacker to implement.
Furthermore, the time window limitation can be overcome by two factors. First, fairly high
bandwidths can be guaranteed between instances in a cloud deployment. For example,
well-optimized N1 machines can achieve up to more than 1 Gbps bandwidths, as described
in this guide. Therefore, a maximum memory allocation of 232 bytes (around 4 GB) can be
easily filled in less than 60 seconds. Secondly, the server does not enforce a single TCP
stream from a given client. This means that the attack can be amplified by creating several
connections, each forcing the server to dedicate 232 bytes, which the malicious client would
then fill to exhaust resources. A group of malicious nodes can further amplify the effect of
this attack.

