We note that the parent task controlling the execution of the entire MPC protocol instance
will time out eventually. The protocol timeout is part of the node configuration and is
typically on the order of 60 seconds. However, there is no log message identifying the
misbehaving node, which makes troubleshooting this type of issue unnecessarily difficult.

The severity of this issue is rated as medium because it could impact the availability of the
group. On the other hand, to carry out the attack, the sender needs to be part of the group,
which implies a high level of privilege. For this reason, the difficulty is rated as high.

Exploit Scenario
A malfunctioning MPC node drops outgoing packets, which causes other nodes to hang
while waiting for incoming data. This causes intermittent protocol failures that are hard for
the NEAR One team to diagnose.

Recommendations:
Short term, use a preconfigured timeout to ensure that the TLS listener thread is not left
waiting for data from malfunctioning or malicious nodes.

Long term, extend the use of logging to make it easier for node operators to investigate
unexpected behavior. Ensure that log messages originating from potentially malicious
behavior always identify the corresponding remote node.

