The Liquidium protocol canister computes current collateral by imputing the current
interest rate backward to cover the whole interval since the last position update. This
allows lenders to artificially increase their profits by briefly manipulating the interest rate
upward and then accruing interest as if the rate were inflated for the full lending period.

The highlighted section of figure 2.1 shows the calculation of the index_ratio value and
includes a comment indicating that this should track the growth of a global borrow index.
However, instead of referencing a global index, the code sets the index_ratio value to
the current spot lending rate. Note that the else branch divides and then multiplies the
current lending_rate value by the lending_rate_snapshot, thus leaving the value
unchanged.

The lending_rate value is computed by a piecewise-linear function of the current pool
supply and does not track interest over time.

Ultimately, this logic applies the current rate linearly across the time since the particular
position was last updated. Borrow-side interest is not guaranteed to surpass supply-side
interest, allowing lenders to drain funds until the protocol is insolvent.

