Finding: Stale prices cannot be updated
Severity: Medium
Difficulty: Low
Type: Data Validation

Description:
The sync_price function incorrectly uses a less-than operator to check whether the
cached price is eligible to be synced, preventing stale prices from being updated.

 async fn sync_price(base: String, quote: String) -> Result<(Nat, u32), String> {
 use crate::price_oracle::exchange_rate_canister::{get_exchange_rate, Asset,
 AssetClass, GetExchangeRateRequest};

 let cache = PRICE_CACHE.with_borrow(|cache| cache.get(&format!("{}{}", base,
 quote)).clone());

 // 1 minute in ns
 const CACHE_EXPIRY_NS: u64 = 60_000_000_000u64;

 if let Some(entry) = cache {
 if entry.2 < time() - CACHE_EXPIRY_NS {
 return Ok((Nat::from_str(&entry.0).unwrap(), entry.1));
 }
 };

 Figure 7.1: An incorrect comparison
 (liquidium-pool/canisters/lending/src/price_oracle/mod.rs#L51–L63)

Exploit Scenario
The protocol is deployed, and the initial prices are fetched and saved to the cache. One day
later, the timer established by the register_price_timer function triggers the next
price update. The saved price has a timestamp from 24 hours ago, which is a smaller value
than the current time minus the cache expiry constant, which is the timestamp from 1
minute ago. Therefore, the sync_prices function will return early instead of syncing
prices on this and all future attempts at syncing prices. The protocol must be upgraded to
resolve this problem.

Recommendations:
Short term, replace the less-than operator highlighted in figure 7.1 with a greater-than
operator.

Long term, ensure that all comparisons are verified by unit tests.

