Finding: Removal of an account iterates over all accounts
Severity: Low
Difficulty: Medium
Type: Denial of Service

Description:
Removal of an account from a user profile requires iterating over all user accounts in the
system. This allows an algorithmic complexity attack that could enable cycle drain attacks
or simply increase the system cost. While nonreplicated query methods can safely iterate
over all users, replicated update methods should avoid doing so.

 pub fn remove_wallet(profile: Principal, req: RemoveAccountRequest) -> Result<(),
 ProtocolError> {
 let account = Account::from(req.wallet_address);

 let wallet = ACCOUNTS
 .with_borrow(|wallets| wallets.get(&(profile, account)).clone())
 .ok_or(ProtocolError::AccountNotFound)?
 .0;

 let accounts = ACCOUNTS.with_borrow(|accounts| accounts.iter().filter(|a| a.0 .0
 == profile).count());

 if accounts == 1 {
 return Err(ProtocolError::CannotRemoveSoleAccount);
 }

 ACCOUNTS.with_borrow_mut(|accounts| {
 accounts.remove(&(profile, account));
 });

 WALLETS.with_borrow_mut(|wallets| {
 wallets.remove(&account);
 });

 Protocol::insert_event(profile, ProtocolEventType::AccountRemoved(wallet));

 Ok(())
 }

 Figure 18.1: Iteration over all accounts in the system
(liquidium-pool/canisters/lending/src/account/account_manager.rs#129–155)

