We did not find any way to exploit the fact that the hash is currently unkeyed, because the
BBOT procedures themselves already include domain separation where possible. However,
adding additional domain separation here should be seen as a defense-in-depth measure.

Recommendations:
Short term, use the session ID or another transcript-derived value as a Blake2b hash key
when converting the BBOT output messages.

Long term, use domain separation when hashing elements wherever possible.

