Finding: ECDSA signature verification does not enforce low s values
Severity: Informational
Difficulty: Not Applicable
Type: Cryptography

Description:
Since the ECDSA verification equation does not change if the sign of 𝑠 is flipped, ECDSA
verification implementations impose a canonical form for signatures by requiring that 𝑠 is
below half the scalar modulus. The final step of the threshold signing algorithm checks that
the generated signature (𝑟, 𝑠) passes verification using the Signature::verify method,
shown in figure 1.1.

 // This verification tests the signature including whether s has been normalized
 pub fn verify(&self, public_key: &AffinePoint, msg_hash: &Scalar) -> bool {
 let r: Scalar = x_coordinate(&self.big_r);
 if r.is_zero().into() || self.s.is_zero().into() {
 return false;
 }
 // tested earlier is not zero, so inversion will not raise an error and unwrap
 cannot panic
 let s_inv = self.s.invert_vartime().unwrap();
 let reproduced = (ProjectivePoint::GENERATOR * (*msg_hash * s_inv))
 + (ProjectivePoint::from(*public_key) * (r * s_inv));
 x_coordinate(&reproduced.into()) == r
 }

 Figure 1.1: ECDSA verification; note that the only check done on s is to ensure it is nonzero
 (threshold-signatures/src/ecdsa/mod.rs#62–73)

This check does not enforce the low 𝑠 requirement. However, as shown in figure 1.2, the 𝑠
value is normalized in the final step of signing regardless, so this missing validation does
not affect the result if the coordinator behaves honestly.

